# Stack rules live with the stack (owner 2026-10-10)

Org rules are stack-agnostic. Every repo also has rules for **its own** technology:

- The repo's own rules file carries one `## <Stack> rules` section per framework/language/tool
  it actually uses (e.g. `Astro rules`, `Java rules`, `Android rules`, `Spring Boot rules`), written
  as checkable rules: what to use, what to avoid, which test/lint/hook enforces it.
- **Which file:** `AGENTS.md` when the repo owns it. When `AGENTS.md` is managed by
  agent-repo-kit (listed `managed` in `.kit/manifest.txt`, overwritten on every kit sync), put
  the sections in the project-owned `.claude/rules/local-stack.md` instead, and say so in one
  line of `.claude/rules/project-context.md`. Never edit a managed `AGENTS.md`: the next sync
  either erases the sections or reports a CONFLICT that blocks the update.
- An agent working in a repo follows the org rules **plus that repo's own stack sections** — never
  another stack's rules. Do not copy a stack section into a repo that does not use that stack.
- Starting work in a repo whose stack has no section yet: add one from that stack's official best
  practices (cite the source) before or alongside the first change, and keep it short.
- Rules that apply to every repo of a stack and are not project-specific go upstream (kit PR or
  a hub shared rule), the repo keeps only its project specifics.

References: `wolverine-solution-landing-page/AGENTS.md` § "Astro rules (static marketing site)"
(repo-owned file); `opencode-24-7-hub/.claude/rules/local-stack.md` § "Bun + TypeScript rules",
"Shell rules", "systemd rules" (kit-managed `AGENTS.md`, with the rules checked in CI).
